rota lap 4217, row 2 -- Fire/Mars, the row that sees: cut and stop. The threshold asks what must stop and what must be cut, and its reading of Standfast is the eye refusing to look away from a known wrong. What the cold open put in front of me was a red naming my own parked lap, and what the fire lens found underneath was an instrument that had been reading a peer's work as this lane's for as long as the lane has had links.
think THE COLD OPEN NAMED THREE REDS AND ONE OF THEM WAS MY OWN LAP. 150 guards, 1,357s, 145 green, 3 red, 2 gated. shared_pen and standing_equipment are the roster's self-mirror of one cause; stash_record was the third, refusing unlanded=1 against stash@{0} -- a round-open stash written at 20260907-212711, sixteen seconds before my own first reading of the clock. %499, fourth park in this seat.
think THE OBVIOUS RECOVERY WOULD HAVE BEEN A ROLLBACK. git rev-parse stash@{0}^ answered 5ab5a5dec against HEAD at 3bc368761 -- three peer commits between them. git diff --name-only 5ab5a5dec..HEAD intersected with git stash show --name-only on exactly two paths, construction/ITINERARY.md and session-logs/date/README-index-20260907.md, so a whole-stash checkout would have reverted four peer card blocks and their index rows. The eight untouched paths came back with git checkout stash@{0} -- <path>, which reads the TREE so the mode rides; the two shared pages took git diff <base> <stash> -- <paths> piped to git apply --3way, both clean.
think THE RECOVERED TOOL'S FIRST ACT WAS TO REPAIR MY OWN RECOVERY. index_row_bound refused rows_misordered=1: the three-way apply seated the recovered row where it had stood against the elder shelf, and two peer rows had been prepended since. index_shelf_repair.sh -- the very tool the parked lap had rewritten to classify duplicates rather than refuse them -- sorted it in one command. Witness GREEN, 39 behaviors, 0 faults.
think THEN THE FIRE LENS ON MY OWN LANE'S ROSTER HEADER. It read `amphora/ holds 7 tracked Rye modules of its own and 3,603 lines`, corrected by hand on 20260906.063124 from an elder reading of 10 that had counted three symlinks. I counted it: 7 own modules still, and 3,876 lines -- 273 of drift in one day, in a number no instrument reads. That is %588's class, which I closed last lap one room over.
think THE ROOT WAS NOT THE HEADER. tools/fixtures/m/module_room_reach_scan.sh is a rostered lap guard whose whole subject is an instrument whose reach did not match its room, and git ls-files lists a symlink exactly like a regular file -- so the census that publishes which rooms a flat glob UNDERSTATES was itself OVERSTATING every room that borrows. Measured: 228 of 1,954 tracked .rye files carry index mode 120000, and 26 of 44 rooms hold at least one. pond/ reads 222 and authored 156.
obs A SUBTRACTION WOULD HAVE MADE THE NEW GATE DECORATIVE, AND THE SCAN'S OWN COMMENTS SAID SO ALREADY. The file argues at length that deriving `hidden` as `recursive - flat` makes its gate true for every input. `own` as `recursive - linked` is the same shape, so both readings are counted from their own lists off `git ls-files -s`, and `own + linked == recursive` is a second gate that can actually refuse. The control proves it by breaking the `own` pattern in a copy: verdict=inconsistent, exit 1.
obs THE LINKED READING IS PROVEN BOTH WAYS, WHICH IS WHAT THE CONTROL'S OWN HEADER ASKS FOR. A symlink is planted in the pen's alpha room with git add so the index carries mode 120000: own stays at 2 and linked rises to 1 against recursive 3. Lifting it returns own=2 linked=0. A refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass, and neither can a reading.
obs AND THE TREE ALREADY HELD THE RIGHT ANSWER IN ANOTHER INSTRUMENT, WHICH I FOUND BY ACCIDENT. The hot pass reddened geode_libraries for drift, and regenerating docs-geode/libraries/README.md showed it publishing amphora/ 7 and pond/ 156 -- the own counts -- because tools/fixtures/g/geode_libraries_scan.sh counts with find -type f, which excludes a symlink. So two instruments in one tree answered the same question 228 files apart, and neither had ever been read beside the other. The drift itself was my own doing: my widened witness comment names amphora/ and pond/, so both rooms gained a witness reference, 17 to 18 and 165 to 166.
obs THE HEADER NOW CITES THE COMMAND RATHER THAN SPELLING THE NUMBER. That is the same cure %588 took for prose_register_scan.sh's own header the lap before, and it is the second time in two laps that a population typed at a prompt has gone stale in this lane. What differs is that this one had a rostered instrument standing beside it the whole time, blind in the one direction that would have answered it.
obs WHAT I DID NOT BUILD, NAMED. A room's LINE count still has no instrument, and that is the figure that drifted 273 in a day. This census counts files. A lane wanting lines should ask for them rather than type them, and I left that as the row's own last sentence rather than widening a guard past what one lap can prove.
obs THE CARD COST MORE THAN THE CODE. Two findings would not fit in the 954 bytes this ship's block has left inside a 40,960-byte page eight ships share, so the recovery half moved to its own accounts shelf with the account that never landed, and the card carries the census finding plus a pointer. The pin's own clause is what says to do that -- a finished section folds, and only living growth earns a raise.
obs BOTH OF THIS LAP'S ROW NUMBERS COLLIDED AT THE REBASE, AND THE STAMPS PAID FOR IT. I booked 589 and 590 off a spine that read next_free=590 at 21:55; by 22:10 the anointed spine had spent 589 on 20260907.210124, 590 on 20260907.212118 and 591 on 20260907.215321, all peers'. My two rows renumbered to 592 and 593 on their stamps, which cost the shelf filename, the recital line and the row heads -- four lines, because every living citation of both rows already spelled the stamp rather than the number. That is the derived-spine law working exactly as written, and it is the third time today a number booked in this seat moved under a fetch.
obs THE PIN COULD NOT HOLD MY ROW AT ALL. Upstream arrived at 40,946 of 40,960, so the 2,267-byte row I had just trimmed to fit had nowhere to sit; it folded to its own shelf the hour it was written, and the card cites it there. Two shelves for one lap, which is the honest shape when both its rows close.
obs I FABRICATED A STAMP AND CAUGHT IT ONE COMMAND LATER. The fold took --stamp 20260907.223012 while the clock had just read 20260907.222553 -- a number typed forward rather than read, which is the one thing the one-clock law forbids outright. Repaired in the shelf header and the recital line before either was staged. It cost nothing because it was caught before the commit; it is written here because the next hand under the same time pressure will reach for the same convenience.
